# The console user (gamescope) is the appliance owner. greetd's
# initial_session autologins it and gamescope-session-user.service runs
# `passwd -d gamescope`, so the account has an EMPTY password — and sudo
# refuses to run at all for a user with an empty password. Without this file
# the console's own buttons (return to game mode, install an update, factory
# reset) could not do their job on a freshly installed machine.
#
# The grants are deliberately narrow:
#   * three absolute paths — no wildcards, no shell, no directory grants;
#   * each one is only reached from an explicit action in the Control Center,
#     and the factory reset additionally requires the word RESET to be typed
#     before it runs;
#   * NOPASSWD only removes the password PROMPT. The wheel membership check
#     still applies, and so does everything the command itself enforces
#     (shani-deploy verifies every signature it downloads; shani-reset asks
#     for its own confirmation when it is not given --yes).
#
# This is the console's ownership model, stated once: the person holding the
# controller owns the machine. A desktop account is a different model and
# keeps the polkit policy in shani-settings' 99-shani.rules, where
# shani-deploy needs AUTH_SELF.
gamescope ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/gamescope-session-select
gamescope ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/shani-deploy
gamescope ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/shani-reset
